1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,558
Ava: If a model already runs, why should a framework engineer care about a conference post full of compiler and kernel talks?
Ava：模型已经能跑了，框架工程师为什么还要关注一篇满是编译器和内核演讲的会议文章？

2
00:00:08,558 --> 00:00:16,272
Brian: Because those layers affect how quickly a model starts, how much memory it needs, and whether devices sit idle.
Brian：因为这些底层技术影响模型启动速度、内存占用，以及设备会不会闲置。

3
00:00:16,272 --> 00:00:20,236
This roundup is about the work underneath the model.
这篇综述讲的是模型底层的工作。

4
00:00:20,236 --> 00:00:28,986
Ava: We're talking about PyTorch Conference North America twenty twenty-six, in San Jose on October twentieth and twenty-first?
Ava：我们说的是10月20日至21日在圣何塞举行的2026年 PyTorch 北美大会？

5
00:00:28,986 --> 00:00:34,526
Brian: Right. The article tours sessions on open research, tooling, and optimization.
Brian：对。文章介绍了开放研究、工具和优化方面的演讲。

6
00:00:34,526 --> 00:00:39,848
It's a guide to talks, so each result belongs to a particular session.
这是一份演讲导览，每项成果都对应一场具体演讲。

7
00:00:39,848 --> 00:00:44,134
Ava: What's the common problem behind so many different talks?
Ava：这么多不同的演讲，共同要解决什么问题？

8
00:00:44,134 --> 00:00:48,302
Brian: PyTorch code has to become efficient work on real hardware.
Brian：要让 PyTorch 代码在实际硬件上高效运行。

9
00:00:48,302 --> 00:00:54,444
That takes compilers, kernels, data movement, and tools that show where time goes.
这需要编译器、内核、数据搬运，以及能看清时间花在哪里的工具。

10
00:00:54,444 --> 00:01:00,674
Ava: Let's start with torch.compile. I know the name, but what does this article focus on?
Ava：先说说 torch.compile。我知道这个名字，但这篇文章主要讲什么？

11
00:01:00,674 --> 00:01:05,668
Brian: It focuses on the machinery that turns eager PyTorch code into a graph.
Brian：重点是把 PyTorch 即时执行的代码转换成计算图的机制。

12
00:01:05,668 --> 00:01:13,264
That graph can be optimized while the program runs or used to generate an artifact for later execution.
程序运行时可以优化这张图，也可以用它生成供以后执行的产物。

13
00:01:13,264 --> 00:01:18,246
Ava: So the compiler needs a useful graph before it can make that code faster.
Ava：所以编译器得先拿到有用的计算图，才能让代码跑得更快。

14
00:01:18,246 --> 00:01:24,078
Brian: Exactly. One talk tackles a graph break, a point where graph capture stops.
Brian：没错。一场演讲讨论了图中断，也就是计算图捕获停止的地方。

15
00:01:24,078 --> 00:01:30,572
Nested breaks can make the compiler repeat work, especially deep inside layers.
嵌套的图中断会让编译器重复工作，尤其是在模型层次很深时。

16
00:01:30,572 --> 00:01:37,306
Ava: Wait, so a small interruption can get expensive just because it's buried deep in the model?
Ava：等等，一个小中断只因藏在模型深处，就会变得很耗时？

17
00:01:37,306 --> 00:01:38,521
Brian: That's the issue.
Brian：问题就在这里。

18
00:01:38,521 --> 00:01:45,024
The new Dynamo support described in the article cuts duplicate breaks to a constant amount.
文章介绍的 Dynamo 新支持把重复的图中断次数降到了常数级。

19
00:01:45,024 --> 00:01:50,448
Frame traces grow linearly with nesting depth instead of quadratically.
帧追踪量随嵌套深度线性增长，而不再是二次增长。

20
00:01:50,448 --> 00:01:53,578
Ava: Dynamo is the part doing this graph capture.
Ava：Dynamo 就是负责捕获计算图的部分。

21
00:01:53,578 --> 00:01:58,766
Less repeated tracing should also make the result easier to inspect.
减少重复追踪，也应该让结果更容易检查。

22
00:01:58,766 --> 00:02:08,164
Brian: That's what the session claims: larger captured graphs, fewer breaks, and compilation that's faster and easier to debug.
Brian：演讲确实这么说：捕获的图更大、中断更少，编译更快，也更容易调试。

23
00:02:08,164 --> 00:02:14,202
Ava: What about shapes? A batch or sequence length can change between calls.
Ava：那形状呢？批量大小或序列长度可能每次调用都不同。

24
00:02:14,202 --> 00:02:15,971
Brian: There are two angles here.
Brian：这里有两个方向。

25
00:02:15,971 --> 00:02:20,460
Pyrefly brings static tensor shape checking into the type checker.
Pyrefly 把静态张量形状检查引入了类型检查器。

26
00:02:20,460 --> 00:02:27,680
The article says shape mismatches cause roughly forty-five percent of deep learning program failures.
文章称，约45%的深度学习程序故障由形状不匹配导致。

27
00:02:27,680 --> 00:02:33,838
Ava: So Pyrefly tries to catch a mismatch before I spend time running or compiling?
Ava：所以 Pyrefly 想在我花时间运行或编译前发现形状不匹配？

28
00:02:33,838 --> 00:02:34,111
Brian: Yes.
Brian：对。

29
00:02:34,111 --> 00:02:43,932
The talk says it was evaluated across twenty-eight real models, including language, vision, recommender, and reinforcement learning models.
演讲称，他们用28个真实模型做了评估，涵盖语言、视觉、推荐和强化学习模型。

30
00:02:43,932 --> 00:02:48,530
Ava: And the other angle is handling shapes that really do change?
Ava：另一个方向是处理确实会变化的形状？

31
00:02:48,530 --> 00:02:52,613
Brian: A session on parametrized CUDA Graphs addresses that.
Brian：一场关于参数化 CUDA Graph 的演讲讨论了这个问题。

32
00:02:52,613 --> 00:02:55,848
A CUDA Graph captures GPU work for replay.
CUDA Graph 会捕获 GPU 任务，以便重复执行。

33
00:02:55,848 --> 00:03:04,672
This approach reuses one captured graph across dynamic shapes, without recording one for every possible shape.
这种方法让一张捕获的图适用于不同的动态形状，无需为每种可能的形状分别录制。

34
00:03:04,672 --> 00:03:10,014
Ava: That's the part I didn't get. How can one recording serve different shapes?
Ava：这点我没明白。一份录制结果怎么能适用于不同形状？

35
00:03:10,014 --> 00:03:17,646
Brian: The article points to torch.compile's symbolic tracing and guards, then says the graph is re-parametrized.
Brian：文章先讲 torch.compile 的符号追踪和守卫机制，再说计算图经过了重参数化。

36
00:03:17,646 --> 00:03:21,452
It doesn't give the implementation details here.
这里没有给出实现细节。

37
00:03:21,452 --> 00:03:27,226
Ava: So far, we're spending less time building graphs and getting more use from each one.
Ava：到目前为止，我们构建计算图花的时间更少，每张图也能发挥更大作用。

38
00:03:27,226 --> 00:03:33,388
Brian: Right. There's also a new C++ FakeTensor, a tensor stand-in used during tracing.
Brian：对。还有一种新的 C++ FakeTensor，是追踪时用来代替真实张量的。

39
00:03:33,388 --> 00:03:40,824
The article says FakeTensor propagation takes roughly twenty percent of Dynamo tracing time.
文章称，FakeTensor 传播约占 Dynamo 追踪时间的20%。

40
00:03:40,824 --> 00:03:47,438
Ava: I saw a thirty-times-faster number. Is that a thirty-times-faster compile?
Ava：我看到一个快30倍的数字。是编译快了30倍吗？

41
00:03:47,438 --> 00:03:55,105
Brian: No. That's the reported speedup on operations such as aten.mm with the new FakeTensor implementation.
Brian：不是。那是新版 FakeTensor 实现让 aten.mm 等算子获得的文中报告的加速效果。

42
00:03:55,105 --> 00:04:02,212
The article says it reduces cold-start compilation time, but gives no overall multiplier.
文章说它缩短了冷启动编译时间，但没有给出整体加速倍数。

43
00:04:02,212 --> 00:04:06,618
Ava: Good distinction. What's next once the graph is ready?
Ava：这个区别很重要。计算图准备好后，下一步是什么？

44
00:04:06,618 --> 00:04:10,567
Brian: Kernels. These are the pieces of work sent to the hardware.
Brian：内核，也就是交给硬件执行的计算任务。

45
00:04:10,567 --> 00:04:15,536
The conference has a whole group of talks on writing and tuning them.
大会有一整组演讲，专门讲如何编写和调优内核。

46
00:04:15,536 --> 00:04:19,990
Ava: I keep seeing DSL. What's that in this context?
Ava：我总看到 DSL。在这里是什么意思？

47
00:04:19,990 --> 00:04:25,359
Brian: It's a domain-specific language: a language focused on a particular job.
Brian：就是领域专用语言，专门用于某类任务的语言。

48
00:04:25,359 --> 00:04:32,196
Here, that job is expressing GPU kernels without writing every hardware detail by hand.
这里的任务是描述 GPU 内核，而不用手写每一项硬件细节。

49
00:04:32,196 --> 00:04:35,522
Ava: And Helion is one of those languages?
Ava：Helion 也是这类语言之一？

50
00:04:35,522 --> 00:04:39,541
Brian: Yes. It's PyTorch's high-level kernel-authoring DSL.
Brian：对。它是 PyTorch 用于编写内核的高级 DSL。

51
00:04:39,541 --> 00:04:51,328
A session describes new backends using CuTe DSL for recent NVIDIA GPUs and Pallas for TPUs, so one kernel source can target very different hardware.
有场分享介绍了新后端：近期的 NVIDIA GPU 使用 CuTe DSL，TPU 使用 Pallas，让同一份内核源码适配差异很大的硬件。

52
00:04:51,328 --> 00:04:53,494
Ava: One recipe, different kitchens.
Ava：同一份菜谱，不同的厨房。

53
00:04:53,494 --> 00:04:57,966
Does the article say the same kernel is fastest everywhere?
文章说同一个内核在所有设备上都最快吗？

54
00:04:57,966 --> 00:05:04,533
Brian: It doesn't. It describes portability, then other talks show why tuning still matters.
Brian：没有。它讲的是可移植性；其他分享则说明调优依然重要。

55
00:05:04,533 --> 00:05:14,540
FlyDSL, for example, joins TorchInductor's matrix multiplication pipeline on AMD GPUs and reports speedups over Triton there.
例如，FlyDSL 接入了 AMD GPU 上的 TorchInductor 矩阵乘法流水线，并报告其速度超过了 Triton。

56
00:05:14,540 --> 00:05:17,756
Ava: So portability gets the kernel onto a device.
Ava：所以，可移植性让内核能在设备上运行。

57
00:05:17,756 --> 00:05:21,250
Autotuning looks for a better way to run it?
自动调优则寻找更好的运行方式？

58
00:05:21,250 --> 00:05:26,271
Brian: Exactly. Autotuning tries configurations and picks promising ones.
Brian：没错。自动调优会尝试不同配置，挑出有潜力的方案。

59
00:05:26,271 --> 00:05:36,360
In Helion, a filter based on Likelihood-Free Bayesian Optimization reportedly cuts tuning time by thirty-six point five percent.
在 Helion 中，据报告，基于 Likelihood-Free Bayesian Optimization 的筛选器将调优时间缩短了 36.5%。

60
00:05:36,360 --> 00:05:39,950
Ava: That's tuning time, not model training time.
Ava：缩短的是调优时间，不是模型训练时间。

61
00:05:39,950 --> 00:05:45,781
Brian: Correct. The same session also describes large-language-model-guided autotuning.
Brian：对。同一场分享还介绍了大语言模型引导的自动调优。

62
00:05:45,781 --> 00:05:54,004
It reports up to ten-times-faster tuning on NVIDIA B two hundred, under the conditions used for that result.
在该结果所采用的条件下，NVIDIA B200 上的调优速度据报告最高提升至 10 倍。

63
00:05:54,004 --> 00:06:00,234
Ava: Are there results for an actual serving kernel, rather than just its tuning process?
Ava：除了调优过程，实际用于推理服务的内核有结果吗？

64
00:06:00,234 --> 00:06:10,695
Brian: An experimental Helion backend for Paged Attention reports up to fifty percent lower latency and ten percent higher end-to-end throughput than an optimized Triton version.
Brian：一个实验性的 Helion Paged Attention 后端报告称，相比优化过的 Triton 版本，延迟最高降低 50%，端到端吞吐量提高 10%。

65
00:06:10,695 --> 00:06:13,064
Those are two different measures.
这是两个不同的指标。

66
00:06:13,064 --> 00:06:19,077
Ava: Let me check I've got this. Better compiler capture gives the kernel more to work with.
Ava：我确认一下理解得对不对：编译器捕获更多计算信息，内核就有更多优化空间。

67
00:06:19,077 --> 00:06:25,510
A DSL helps write kernels for different devices. Tuning still decides how well they run.
DSL 帮助为不同设备编写内核，调优则决定它们跑得有多好。

68
00:06:25,510 --> 00:06:27,052
Brian: That's a fair recap.
Brian：总结得很准确。

69
00:06:27,052 --> 00:06:32,724
Now imagine doing all of that across a cluster instead of one device.
现在想象一下，把这一切从单台设备扩展到整个集群。

70
00:06:32,724 --> 00:06:38,618
Ava: This is where sharding comes in: splitting model data across devices?
Ava：这就要用到分片了：把模型数据分到不同设备上？

71
00:06:38,618 --> 00:06:38,906
Brian: Yes.
Brian：对。

72
00:06:38,906 --> 00:06:47,992
FlexShard is a prototype that separates parameter sharding from the runtime machinery of FSDP, or Fully Sharded Data Parallel.
FlexShard 是一个原型，将参数分片与 FSDP（完全分片数据并行）的运行时机制分开。

73
00:06:47,992 --> 00:06:54,904
Its small Placement contract can support optimizer-aware and quantization-aligned sharding.
它用简洁的 Placement 契约支持适配优化器、对齐量化需求的分片方式。

74
00:06:54,904 --> 00:06:57,510
Ava: Why separate those pieces?
Ava：为什么要把这两部分分开？

75
00:06:57,510 --> 00:07:02,174
Brian: Different optimizers and data formats may want different placements.
Brian：不同的优化器和数据格式可能需要不同的数据放置方式。

76
00:07:02,174 --> 00:07:10,412
The article gives matrix optimizers such as Muon and block-wise eight-bit floating point communication as examples.
文章举了 Muon 等矩阵优化器，以及分块式 8 位浮点通信的例子。

77
00:07:10,412 --> 00:07:15,610
Ava: And compilation across a cluster can be duplicated too, right?
Ava：整个集群的编译工作也可能重复，对吧？

78
00:07:15,610 --> 00:07:16,036
Brian: Right.
Brian：对。

79
00:07:16,036 --> 00:07:24,262
A talk on precompiling distributed training programs proposes compiling once and reusing the artifact on every rank.
一场关于分布式训练程序预编译的分享提出，只编译一次，再让每个 rank 复用编译产物。

80
00:07:24,262 --> 00:07:31,704
That removes redundant per-rank compilation and keeps kernel selection identical across the cluster.
这样能省去各 rank 重复编译，并让整个集群选择相同的内核。

81
00:07:31,704 --> 00:07:35,953
Ava: It also mentions divergent compiler decisions causing hangs.
Ava：文章还提到，编译器决策不一致可能导致程序卡住。

82
00:07:35,953 --> 00:07:39,206
That's an expensive kind of disagreement.
这种分歧的代价可不小。

83
00:07:39,206 --> 00:07:39,612
Brian: It is.
Brian：确实。

84
00:07:39,612 --> 00:07:48,892
Another session, AutoParallel, searches the model graph for a training strategy from a device mesh, memory budget, and sample batch.
另一场分享介绍了 AutoParallel：它根据设备网格、内存预算和样本批次，在模型图中搜索训练策略。

85
00:07:48,892 --> 00:07:52,722
Ava: Does the article give a concrete result for that search?
Ava：文章给出了这种搜索的具体结果吗？

86
00:07:52,722 --> 00:08:00,877
Brian: It says AutoParallel matched a strong hand-tuned baseline on Llama three eight billion while using twelve percent less memory.
Brian：文章说，在 Llama 3 8B 上，AutoParallel 达到了优秀的人工调优基线的水平，同时少用了 12% 的内存。

87
00:08:00,877 --> 00:08:05,240
That's the reported comparison, not a promise for every model.
这是报告中的对比结果，不代表所有模型都能如此。

88
00:08:05,240 --> 00:08:11,422
Ava: What happens below the sharding strategy, where devices actually exchange data?
Ava：分片策略之下，设备实际交换数据时又是怎么做的？

89
00:08:11,422 --> 00:08:13,772
Brian: The communication talks go there.
Brian：通信相关的分享讲的就是这个。

90
00:08:13,772 --> 00:08:24,588
PyTorch Symmetric Memory gives each rank a direct window into peers' GPU memory, so communication can happen inside a kernel alongside computation.
PyTorch Symmetric Memory 让每个 rank 都能直接访问其他 rank 的 GPU 内存窗口，因此内核可以一边计算一边通信。

91
00:08:24,588 --> 00:08:31,010
Ava: Like reaching across the table for an ingredient while cooking, instead of stopping to arrange a delivery?
Ava：就像做饭时直接伸手从桌子另一边拿食材，不用停下来安排配送？

92
00:08:31,010 --> 00:08:34,520
Brian: That's the idea. Other talks address failures.
Brian：就是这个意思。其他分享还讨论了故障处理。

93
00:08:34,520 --> 00:08:45,400
MCCL, the Meta Collective Communication Library, can reconfigure communication as ranks fail or recover, without restarting the whole job.
MCCL（Meta Collective Communication Library）能在 rank 故障或恢复时重新配置通信，无需重启整个任务。

94
00:08:45,400 --> 00:08:53,190
Ava: So the cluster story is placement, consistent compilation, faster communication, and recovery.
Ava：所以集群这一块包括数据放置、一致的编译、更快的通信和故障恢复。

95
00:08:53,190 --> 00:08:56,994
Brian: Yes. The article also covers low-precision training.
Brian：对。文章还介绍了低精度训练。

96
00:08:56,994 --> 00:09:02,612
That's where performance gains have to be weighed against model quality.
这方面需要权衡性能提升与模型质量。

97
00:09:02,612 --> 00:09:07,306
Ava: Take NVFP4. What should I hear in that name?
Ava：以 NVFP4 为例，这个名字说明了什么？

98
00:09:07,306 --> 00:09:09,725
Brian: It's a four-bit floating point format.
Brian：它是一种 4 位浮点格式。

99
00:09:09,725 --> 00:09:19,944
The article says NVFP4 matrix multiplication has two to three times the peak throughput of eight-bit floating point on GB two hundred and GB three hundred.
文章称，在 GB200 和 GB300 上，NVFP4 矩阵乘法的峰值吞吐量是八位浮点格式的两到三倍。

100
00:09:19,944 --> 00:09:23,244
Ava: Peak throughput sounds like a hardware figure.
Ava：峰值吞吐量听起来是硬件指标。

101
00:09:23,244 --> 00:09:26,678
Does training quality follow automatically?
训练质量也会随之提升吗？

102
00:09:26,678 --> 00:09:34,925
Brian: No. The article explicitly notes quality gaps against BF16, or bfloat sixteen, at smaller model sizes.
Brian：不是。文章明确指出，较小模型与 BF16（即 bfloat16）相比仍有质量差距。

103
00:09:34,925 --> 00:09:40,612
A session presents recipe improvements intended to close that gap.
一场报告介绍了旨在缩小这一差距的训练配方改进。

104
00:09:40,612 --> 00:09:45,330
Ava: There's also HiFloat. Is its claim framed differently?
Ava：还有 HiFloat。它的说法有什么不同？

105
00:09:45,330 --> 00:09:45,591
Brian: Yes.
Brian：对。

106
00:09:45,591 --> 00:09:51,394
Its eight-bit and four-bit formats are designed around weight and gradient distributions.
它的八位和四位格式是针对权重与梯度的分布设计的。

107
00:09:51,394 --> 00:10:00,872
The session reports loss parity with sixteen-bit floating point and a one point five to one point seven times matrix multiplication speedup.
报告称，它的损失与十六位浮点格式相当，矩阵乘法速度提升了 1.5 到 1.7 倍。

108
00:10:00,872 --> 00:10:08,782
Ava: The distinction is useful: peak hardware throughput, kernel speed, and training loss are separate things.
Ava：这个区分很有用：硬件峰值吞吐量、内核速度和训练损失是三回事。

109
00:10:08,782 --> 00:10:12,847
Brian: Exactly. And the performance story isn't limited to training.
Brian：没错。而且性能提升不只发生在训练阶段。

110
00:10:12,847 --> 00:10:22,260
One serving talk treats checkpoint loading as data movement and reports four point eight to seven point five times faster model loading.
一场关于模型服务的报告把检查点加载视为数据搬运问题，并称模型加载速度提升了 4.8 到 7.5 倍。

111
00:10:22,260 --> 00:10:26,593
Ava: Another talks about LMCache, the cluster-wide prompt cache.
Ava：另一场报告谈到集群级提示词缓存 LMCache。

112
00:10:26,593 --> 00:10:29,954
That's about reusing work during serving?
它是用来在模型服务时复用已有计算结果的吗？

113
00:10:29,954 --> 00:10:30,224
Brian: Yes.
Brian：对。

114
00:10:30,224 --> 00:10:38,052
The article describes it as an open-source caching solution spanning several inference engines and storage backends.
文章称，它是一种开源缓存方案，支持多个推理引擎和存储后端。

115
00:10:38,052 --> 00:10:41,776
It doesn't give a speedup number for that session.
文章没有给出那场报告的加速数据。

116
00:10:41,776 --> 00:10:46,470
Ava: And how do engineers decide which layer needs attention first?
Ava：工程师怎么判断该先优化哪一层？

117
00:10:46,470 --> 00:10:47,366
Brian: They measure.
Brian：靠测量。

118
00:10:47,366 --> 00:10:54,601
There's a session on reading PyTorch profiler traces to tell overhead-bound work from compute-bound work.
有一场报告讲如何解读 PyTorch 性能分析记录，区分开销瓶颈和计算瓶颈。

119
00:10:54,601 --> 00:11:01,772
TorchInsights estimates peak memory and runtime for distributed setups before using a real cluster.
TorchInsights 能在使用真实集群前，估算分布式配置的峰值内存占用和运行时间。

120
00:11:01,772 --> 00:11:03,516
Ava: That sounds promising.
Ava：听起来很有前景。

121
00:11:03,516 --> 00:11:09,970
The post also mentions tools people can build on, like TinyTorch and Pyrefly.
文章还提到了 TinyTorch 和 Pyrefly 等可供开发者使用的工具。

122
00:11:09,970 --> 00:11:16,362
Brian: It does. TinyTorch teaches framework internals by rebuilding basic components in Python.
Brian：是的。TinyTorch 通过用 Python 重建基础组件，帮助人们理解框架内部原理。

123
00:11:16,362 --> 00:11:24,120
Other sessions discuss open tools and how AI agents are changing PyTorch contribution and review work.
其他报告讨论了开放工具，以及 AI 智能体如何改变 PyTorch 的贡献和代码审查工作。

124
00:11:24,120 --> 00:11:29,942
Ava: Where does that leave us on the reported speedups? Should I expect them on my own model?
Ava：那该怎么看这些加速数据？我自己的模型也能达到吗？

125
00:11:29,942 --> 00:11:36,708
Brian: The post summarizes talks, but doesn't provide full methods or workload details for every number.
Brian：文章只是概述了这些报告，没有为每个数字都提供完整的方法或工作负载细节。

126
00:11:36,708 --> 00:11:42,220
We'd need the individual sessions to judge how well each result transfers.
要判断结果能否迁移到自己的场景，还得看各场报告。

127
00:11:42,220 --> 00:11:51,330
Ava: First recap point: compiler work aims to capture more useful graphs and reduce the time spent tracing or compiling them.
Ava：第一点总结：编译器方面的工作旨在捕获更多有用的计算图，并缩短追踪和编译时间。

128
00:11:51,330 --> 00:11:58,520
Brian: Second: kernel languages and tuning help turn those graphs into efficient work on different hardware.
Brian：第二点：内核语言和调优能让这些计算图在不同硬件上高效运行。

129
00:11:58,520 --> 00:12:08,134
Ava: Third: at scale, placement, communication, numerical quality, loading, and measurement all affect the result.
Ava：第三点：在大规模场景下，任务放置、通信、数值质量、加载和测量都会影响结果。

130
00:12:08,134 --> 00:12:13,740
Brian: That's our tour of the work underneath PyTorch. Thanks for listening.
Brian：以上就是我们对 PyTorch 底层工作的梳理。感谢收听。
